      {
        "attempt": 1,
        "status": "OK",
        "duration_seconds": 58.561,
        "asr_text": "In other words, the sound seemed to catch their attention automatically; they did not make a clear decision to stop working. Their minds reacted first. But the story does not end with sound. A second study, published in 2023, looked at a silent phone. 42 university students took a concentration test. Their phones were turned off and no notifications arrived. When the phone was on the desk, the average attention score was about 100. When the phone was moved to another room, it rose to about 109. Their speed score showed a similar difference, about 98 with the phone on the desk, compared with about 109 when the phone was away. The researchers found this lower performance even without a sound or a screen. They suggested that the phone’s presence may leave part of the mind ready for a possible message.",
        "asr_text_length": 809,
        "max_len": 876,
        "substring_ok": null,
        "length_ok": true,
        "phonetic_verdict": null,
        "audio_classification": "HIGH_SIMILARITY_SAFE",
